course: reflection
lesson: 32
title: Olympiad: thinking traps and what helps first ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Thinking traps are thoughts that feel like facts but bend the truth, and they are loudest at night: "always" and "never" thinking ("I ALWAYS mess up"), worst-case thinking (one bad mark becomes "I'll fail everything"), mind reading ("they all think I'm weird"), fortune telling ("the new school will be awful") and blaming yourself for things you didn't control ("Mum and Dad split up because of me").
explain: Each trap has a test question: "Is it really always?", "What's most likely, not worst?", "Did they say that, or am I guessing?", "Can I know the future?", "Was it mine to control?"; a trapped thought usually fails its test, while a true worry passes it and deserves a plan.
explain: Picture Amira after she missed the penalty in the cup final: at 11 p.m. she thinks, "Everyone hates me, I've ruined the whole season, I'm useless at football"; that is three traps in one sentence (mind reading, worst-case and all-or-nothing), and the true version is smaller: "I missed one penalty, I feel awful, and we lost a match."
explain: When something hard happens, help usually comes in an order: first be safe and get calm (slow breathing, a drink of water, a grown-up nearby), then tell someone you trust what happened, then put right what can be put right, then take small steps back to normal life, and over time look back at what you learned.
explain: Not every hard thought is a trap: "I miss Grandad" is simply true, and so is "I was unkind to Leo"; the aim is not to argue with true sadness or fair guilt, but to stop the traps from piling extra pain on top.
fact: A thinking trap is a habit of thought that bends the truth, making things seem worse, bigger or more certain than they are.
fact: All-or-nothing thinking uses words like "always", "never", "everyone" and "nobody"; one example the other way is usually enough to show it is false.
fact: Worst-case thinking, also called catastrophising, jumps from a small problem to the most terrible outcome; asking "what is most likely?" helps.
fact: Mind reading means being sure you know what others think without asking them.
fact: Fortune telling means being sure how the future will turn out, usually badly, though nobody can know the future.
fact: Children are never to blame when their parents argue, separate or divorce; those are grown-up problems and grown-up decisions.
fact: Worries usually feel bigger at night, when the mind is tired and there is nothing else to think about; many look smaller in the morning.
fact: After something scary or upsetting, the first step is to be safe and calm down; breathing out more slowly than you breathe in can help the body settle.
fact: Telling a trusted adult, such as a parent, a teacher, a school nurse or a counsellor, is the step that opens most other help.
fact: Making things right means saying sorry and, where possible, repairing what was damaged; it usually goes best once the first big feelings have calmed a little.
fact: Feelings are not facts: feeling stupid does not make you stupid, and feeling guilty does not always mean you did something wrong.
fact: Some sad thoughts are true, like missing someone who has died; they are not traps, and grief has no timetable.
fact: If sadness or worry stays for weeks and gets in the way of sleeping, eating or school, it is a sign to get extra help from a grown-up or a doctor.
exercise: Amira thinks, "Everyone hates me, I've ruined the season, I'm useless at football." How many thinking traps are in that one sentence? | Three | One | Two | Seven
exercise: Which thought is NOT a thinking trap? | "I miss Grandad and I feel sad today" | "Nobody will ever like me again, ever" | "They're all laughing at me, I just know it" | "Mum and Dad split up because I was naughty"
exercise: Odd one out: three of these are the same trap. Which one is a different trap? | "The new school will be awful, I just know it" | "I always lose at every single game" | "I never get anything right" | "Nobody ever picks me for teams at break time"
exercise: Kai is nipped by a dog in the park; it's not deep. Which order of help is best? | Get away safe, tell an adult, get it checked, later talk about the fear | Talk about the fear first, then get away from the dog, then tell an adult | Get it checked next week, and tell nobody in the meantime | Tell an adult tomorrow, then get away from the dog
exercise: Ruby's parents are splitting up. She thinks, "If I'd been tidier, they'd have stayed together." Which test question breaks this trap? | "Was it mine to control?" | "What's most likely, not worst?" | "Can I really know the future?" | "Is it always?"
exercise: Sam told a lie about Tom and it hurt him. Sam thinks, "I was unfair to Tom." What should he do with this thought? | Use it to say sorry and put things right | Argue it away as a trap | Keep it secret and replay it every night in bed | Decide he is a bad person forever and ever
exercise: Leah got 4 out of 20 in spelling and thinks, "I'll fail every test for the rest of my life." Which is the "most likely" version? | "One test went badly; practice can make the next one better" | "I'll surely get 20 out of 20 next time without even trying" | "Spelling doesn't matter, so the mark doesn't matter either" | "I'll fail them all anyway, so there's no point in trying"
exercise: Odd one out: which of these does NOT help FIRST after a frightening moment? | Replaying every detail in your head | Breathing out slowly | Getting to a safe place | Finding a grown-up nearby
exercise: Check the claim: "If I feel guilty, I must have done something wrong." | False: feelings aren't facts, so check what really happened | True: the feeling of guilt always means that you did something wrong | True, but only if the guilty feeling lasts for a week | False: guilt is never connected to what you have done
exercise: At 2 a.m. Josh thinks, "Tomorrow's assembly will be a disaster and everyone will laugh." At breakfast it feels smaller. Why? | Night makes worries feel bigger | The assembly got cancelled | Breakfast food stops all worries completely | He forgot all about the assembly overnight
exercise: Mira is sure "Jess didn't wave because she hates me now." What is the fairest next step? | Ask Jess, or wait for more clues before deciding | Ignore Jess forever, to get in first | Tell everyone at school that Jess hates her | Decide it's true, because it feels so true
exercise: Tom has missed his grandad for three months. His uncle says, "You should be over it by now." What is true? | Grief has no timetable, and missing him is not a trap | Three months is exactly when grief is meant to end | Tom's sadness is a thinking trap he must argue away | Missing someone that long means something is wrong with you
exercise: Put the usual order of help: P put right, S safe and calm, T tell someone, L look back. | S, T, P, L | T, S, L, P | P, L, S, T | L, S, T, P
exercise: Which sign means it's time for extra help from a doctor or counsellor, and not just time? | Sadness for weeks that stops sleep and eating | Feeling down for an afternoon | Being nervous on the night before a big spelling test | Crying once at a sad film about a dog who gets lost
exercise: After breaking a vase, Ollie wails, "I'm SO dead, Mum's gonna kill me." What does he really mean? | He's scared of getting into trouble | He thinks he is in real danger from his mum | He has already been badly hurt by the vase | He doesn't care about the vase at all
test: A boy from Liverpool says, "Our kid says I'm proper useless at maths cos I got one sum wrong." ("Our kid" means brother or sister.) What is wrong with the brother's claim? | It's all-or-nothing: one wrong sum doesn't make you useless | It's fortune telling about next year's maths | It's a fair fact, since one wrong sum proves he's useless at maths | It's grief, because maths can make you sad
test: Bea says, "I'm literally never gonna have friends at secondary, it's so over." Which test question fits her thought best? | "Can I really know the future?" | "Was it mine to control?" | "Is this a true sadness to accept?" | "What did they actually say to me?"
test: What would happen if Ruby kept believing the split was her fault? | She'd carry guilt for something that was never hers | Her parents would get back together sooner | She would feel better and better each week | Nothing; the thought is true, so it is fine
test: In a cartoon the hero breaks a window and hides it all film. In real life, after Finn's ball breaks a neighbour's window, which order is wisest? | Calm down, tell a grown-up, say sorry, help fix it | Hide the ball, wait, and hope nobody ever notices | Say sorry first, then hide, then deny it all | Run home, blame a friend, then say sorry later
test: A Kiwi mate says, "Yeah nah, I'm sweet as, just a bit gutted about Nan," while Nan is in hospital. ("Sweet as" means fine; "gutted" means very sad.) Which part shows the real feeling? | "gutted about Nan" | "Yeah nah" | "I'm sweet as" | "just a bit"
test: At her dog's funeral in the garden, Mia's cousin snaps, "Shut up, you're ALWAYS crying, you baby." Which word is the trap? | "always": she is crying about a real loss | "crying": crying at a funeral is wrong | "baby": she is young, so it's a fair word | None: the cousin is completely right
test: Check the claim: "Thinking positive means pretending nothing bad happened." | False: you can be sad and still see the true size of things | True: you must smile and look happy whatever happens | True, but only when you are at school with friends | False, because thinking positive is always bad for you
test: Dev from Mumbai says, "Exam's preponed to Monday, I'll surely fail, my life is finished." ("Preponed" means moved earlier.) Which is the true-sized version? | "Less time is stressful, but one exam won't finish my life" | "Monday is fine, because exams never matter at all, not one bit" | "My whole life really is finished, that's a fact" | "I should just hide at home on Monday morning"
test: Zoe sobs, "I've ruined everything a hundred times this year." Her friend counts: 2 bad moments. Which number tells the truth? | 2 | 100 | 50 | 102
test: Grace from Boston says, "Wicked bad day, I'm the worst friend ever." She forgot one birthday. ("Wicked" means very.) What is the fair version? | "I forgot one birthday; I can say sorry and make it up" | "I'm the worst friend in the whole wide world" | "Birthdays don't matter, so it doesn't matter" | "My friend will never talk to me again"
test: Which thought should come FIRST in a good plan after a scary dream? | "I'm safe in bed; I'll breathe slowly" | "I'll replay the dream so I remember all of it" | "Dreams come true, so I must stay awake all night" | "I'll keep this to myself for ever and ever"
exam: Identify the thinking trap shown by the assertion "Everybody in the class must consider me foolish," made in the absence of any statement to that effect. | Mind reading, the assumption of others' thoughts without enquiry | Fortune telling, the confident prediction of future events | Accurate perception, requiring no further examination | Grief, being a natural response to bereavement
exam: Determine the steps which should ordinarily precede the making of amends after a distressing incident. | Attaining calm and informing a trusted adult | Undertaking full repair before any feelings have subsided | Concealing the incident until such time as it is forgotten | Reviewing the incident at length during the night
exam: In respect of a child's belief that he caused his parents' separation, select the statement consistent with the established facts. | Children bear no responsibility for the separation of their parents | Children are partially responsible where they have misbehaved at home or at school | Such beliefs are invariably accurate | Separation results principally from untidiness at home
exam: Which of the following is NOT a thinking trap? | Sorrow at a grandparent's death, months after the event | The conviction that one will fail every future examination | The assertion that nobody will ever befriend one | The belief that a single error proves total incompetence
